# BINARY SEARCH — THEORY

## 1. What is Binary Search?

Binary Search is a searching algorithm used to find an element in a sorted array.

It works by repeatedly dividing the search space into two halves until the target element is found or the search space becomes empty.

Example:

Array: `{10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60}`

Target: `40`

Binary Search checks the middle element and decides whether to search in the left half or the right half.

## 2. Important Condition

Binary Search requires a sorted array.

Example of a sorted array:

```java
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
```

If the array is unsorted, standard Binary Search will not work correctly.

## 3. Important Variables

Binary Search uses three main variables:

1. `s` → Starting index of the search range.
2. `e` → Ending index of the search range.
3. `mid` → Middle index of the search range.

Example:

```java
int s = 0;
int e = arr.length - 1;
int mid = s + (e - s) / 2;
```

## 4. Why do we use arr.length - 1?

Java arrays use 0-based indexing.

Example:

```text
Array:  10  20  30  40  50
Index:   0   1   2   3   4
```

The last valid index is `arr.length - 1`.

Therefore:

```java
int e = arr.length - 1;
```

## 5. Middle Index Formula

The formula to calculate the middle index is:

```java
int mid = s + (e - s) / 2;
```

Example:

```text
s = 0
e = 6

mid = 0 + (6 - 0) / 2
mid = 3
```

The middle index is 3.

This formula helps prevent integer overflow when the indices are very large.

## 6. Three Main Conditions

### Condition 1: Target Found

```java
if (arr[mid] == target) {
    return mid;
}
```

If the middle element is equal to the target, the element has been found.

### Condition 2: Search in the Left Half

```java
else if (arr[mid] > target) {
    e = mid - 1;
}
```

If the middle element is greater than the target, search in the left half.

### Condition 3: Search in the Right Half

```java
else {
    s = mid + 1;
}
```

If the middle element is smaller than the target, search in the right half.

## 7. Why do we use while (s <= e)?

```java
while (s <= e)
```

This condition means that the search continues while the search range is valid.

Even if only one element remains, the algorithm must check it.

If we use `s < e`, we might skip checking the last remaining element.

## 8. Complete Binary Search Code

```java
public class BinarySearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60};
        int target = 40;

        int s = 0;
        int e = arr.length - 1;

        while (s <= e) {

            int mid = s + (e - s) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                System.out.println("Found at index " + mid);
                return;
            }
            else if (arr[mid] > target) {
                e = mid - 1;
            }
            else {
                s = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Not Found");
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Found at index 3
```

## 9. Time Complexity

Best Case: O(1)

Average Case: O(log n)

Worst Case: O(log n)

Space Complexity: O(1) for iterative Binary Search.

## 10. Why is Binary Search O(log n)?

Binary Search divides the array into two halves at every step.

Example:

```text
1000 → 500 → 250 → 125 → 62 → 31 → ...
```

Since the search space is reduced by half each time, Binary Search takes O(log n) time.

## 11. Binary Search vs Linear Search

| Feature               | Linear Search     | Binary Search       |
| --------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| Sorted array required | No                | Yes                 |
| Method                | Checks one by one | Divides into halves |
| Worst-case time       | O(n)              | O(log n)            |
| Space complexity      | O(1)              | O(1)                |

## 12. Common Mistakes

1. Applying Binary Search to an unsorted array.
2. Using `arr.length` instead of `arr.length - 1`.
3. Forgetting to update `s` or `e`.
4. Using `s < e` when the last element also needs to be checked.
5. Confusing `mid` (index) with `arr[mid]` (element).
6. Forgetting `mid + 1` or `mid - 1`, which may cause an infinite loop.

